# Inertia.js Development Patterns

Practical patterns for Laravel + Inertia + Vue work in this repository.

## Version Snapshot

- Laravel 12.55.1
- inertiajs/inertia-laravel 2.0.21
- @inertiajs/vue3 2.3.18
- Vue 3.5.30

## Project Reality Overrides (Highest Priority)

- Follow touched-module conventions first.
- Inertia is the page/navigation bridge; `axios` may still be used for local module actions.
- Vue Options API is the preferred default for new module/page work.
- Preserve existing `<script setup lang="ts">` patterns in files already using them.
- Current app boot resolves pages from `resources/js/pages/**/*.vue` in `resources/js/app.ts`.
- Do not force migrations between layout patterns unless explicitly requested.

## Documentation-First Workflow

1. Use Laravel Boost `search-docs` first for Inertia/Laravel behavior.
2. Use Context7 for supplemental Inertia/Vue examples.
3. If docs and local module conventions diverge, keep local conventions and evolve incrementally.

## Core Inertia Flow

Use redirect-based server responses for mutations and Inertia page renders for navigation.

```php
public function index(): \Inertia\Response
{
    return Inertia::render('settings/Profile', [
        'user' => request()->user()?->only(['id', 'name', 'email']),
    ]);
}

public function update(UpdateProfileRequest $request): \Illuminate\Http\RedirectResponse
{
    $request->user()->fill($request->validated())->save();

    return to_route('profile.edit')->with('success', 'Profile updated.');
}
```

## Server-Side Patterns (Laravel)

### Render Minimal, Explicit Props

- Select only required columns.
- Eager-load only needed relationships.
- Paginate large lists.
- Keep page props small and stable.

### Mutation Responses

- Prefer `to_route(...)` / redirects after `store/update/delete`.
- Let Inertia handle validation error redirects automatically.
- Avoid returning mixed JSON payloads from routes intended to render Inertia pages.

### Shared Props (`HandleInertiaRequests`)

- Keep shared props lightweight (`auth`, flash, small app metadata).
- Use lazy closures for expensive shared values.
- Do not share sensitive values or oversized objects globally.

### Deferred and Partial Data (v2)

- Use `Inertia::defer(...)` for expensive secondary data loads.
- Use partial reload-friendly prop design for frequently refreshed sections.

```php
return Inertia::render('Reports/Index', [
    'reports' => Report::query()->latest()->paginate(20),
    'analytics' => Inertia::defer(fn () => $this->analyticsService->summary()),
]);
```

## Client-Side Page Patterns (Vue Options API Preferred)

Use `Head`, `Link`, and `router` from `@inertiajs/vue3` for navigation/stateful visits.

```vue
<script>
import { defineComponent } from 'vue';
import { Head, Link, router } from '@inertiajs/vue3';

export default defineComponent({
    components: { Head, Link },

    props: {
        users: { type: Object, required: true },
        filters: { type: Object, default: () => ({}) },
    },

    data() {
        return {
            localFilters: { ...this.filters },
        };
    },

    methods: {
        applyFilters() {
            router.get('/users', this.localFilters, {
                only: ['users', 'filters'],
                preserveState: true,
                preserveScroll: true,
            });
        },
    },
});
</script>
```

## Form Patterns

Follow local module conventions: use `useForm`, `<Form>`, or existing validated `axios` flow where appropriate.

### `useForm` (Programmatic Control)

```vue
<script>
import { defineComponent } from 'vue';
import { useForm } from '@inertiajs/vue3';

export default defineComponent({
    setup() {
        const form = useForm({
            name: '',
            email: '',
        });

        return { form };
    },

    methods: {
        submit() {
            this.form.post('/users', {
                preserveScroll: true,
                onSuccess: () => this.form.reset('email'),
            });
        },
    },
});
</script>
```

### `<Form>` Component (Declarative)

- Use for simple declarative form flows.
- Use slot state (`errors`, `processing`, `wasSuccessful`) for UX feedback.
- Use `options.only/except/reset` for partial reload behavior control.

## Navigation and Reload Patterns

- Use `<Link>` for navigation links.
- Use `router.get/post/put/patch/delete/visit/reload` for programmatic flows.
- Use `only` / `except` / `reset` for partial reload optimization on same page component.
- Use `preserveScroll`/`preserveState` deliberately, not by default everywhere.

```js
router.reload({ only: ['users'] });
router.get('/users', { search: 'john' }, { only: ['users', 'filters'] });
```

## Deferred, Visibility, and Infinite Loading (v2)

- Use `Deferred` for deferred props client rendering fallback.
- Use `WhenVisible`/`InfiniteScroll` for lazy loading sections when appropriate.
- Ensure fallback states are explicit (skeleton/loading/empty/error) to avoid layout jumps.

```vue
<template>
    <Deferred data="analytics">
        <template #fallback>
            <div>Loading analytics...</div>
        </template>
        <AnalyticsPanel :data="analytics" />
    </Deferred>
</template>
```

## Inertia + Axios Coexistence

- Use Inertia for page navigation and mutation flows expecting redirect/validation behavior.
- Use `axios` for local widget/table interactions that should not trigger page visits.
- Do not replace standard page navigation with ad-hoc `fetch`/`axios` calls.

## Layout and Page Resolution

- Respect current page resolver convention from `resources/js/app.ts`:
    - `./pages/${name}.vue`
- Keep layout usage consistent with touched files (`AppLayout`, settings layout wrappers, etc.).
- Avoid forcing global-default layout assumptions where module-level layout wrappers exist.

## Testing Patterns (Pest + Inertia)

Use Inertia assertions for page shape and advanced v2 reload behavior.

```php
it('renders users index', function () {
    $this->get('/users')
        ->assertOk()
        ->assertInertia(fn ($page) => $page
            ->component('Users/Index')
            ->has('users')
        );
});
```

- Test partial reloads with `reloadOnly(...)` / `reloadExcept(...)`.
- Test deferred props with `loadDeferredProps(...)` when deferred behavior is introduced.

## Performance Guardrails

- Keep page props lean; avoid sending full models/relations unnecessarily.
- Use pagination for lists.
- Use partial reloads for high-frequency filter/search interactions.
- Avoid globally shared props that are expensive to compute on every request.

## Common Pitfalls

- Mixing `router` and legacy `this.$inertia` styles in the same file.
- Returning JSON from routes that should return `Inertia::render(...)`.
- Overusing `preserveState` and accidentally masking stale UI state.
- Sending heavy data in shared props instead of page/deferred props.
- Ignoring fallback UI for deferred/visibility-based loading.

## Completion Checklist

- [ ] Uses module-consistent Inertia style (`router`, `Link`, `useForm`/`<Form>` as appropriate).
- [ ] Uses redirect-based mutation flow with server-side validation.
- [ ] Keeps props explicit, minimal, and performance-conscious.
- [ ] Uses partial/deferred reload features only when beneficial and tested.
- [ ] Preserves existing layout and file-style conventions in touched modules.
